Finding the Original Client
Problem
You need to find the original client IP address because there are proxies in
front of the NGINX server.
You may find that if you’re using a proxy in front of NGINX, NGINX will
pick up the proxy’s IP address rather than the client’s. For this you can use
the geoip_proxy directive to instruct NGINX to use the X-Forwarded-For
header when connections are opened from a given range. The geoip_proxy
directive takes an address or a CIDR range. When there are multiple proxies
passing traffic in front of NGINX, you can use the geoip_proxy_recursive
directive to recursively search through X-Forwarded-For addresses to find
the originating client. You will want to use something like this when utilizing
load balancers such as Amazon Web Services Elastic Load Balancing (AWS
ELB), Google’s load balancer, or Microsoft Azure’s load balancer in front of
NGINX




load_module "/usr/lib64/nginx/modules/ngx_http_geoip_module.so";

http {

    geoip_country /etc/nginx/geoip/GeoIP.dat;
    geoip_city /etc/nginx/geoip/GeoLiteCity.dat;

    # بازه IP لودبالنسر / پروکسی
    geoip_proxy 10.0.0.0/8;
    geoip_proxy 192.168.0.0/16;
    geoip_proxy_recursive on;

    log_format geoip_log
        '$remote_addr '
        'xff=$http_x_forwarded_for '
        'country=$geoip_country_code '
        'city=$geoip_city';

    access_log /var/log/nginx/access.log geoip_log;

    server {
        listen 443 ssl;
        server_name example.com;

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://backend_app;
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        }
    }
}



